TOPOLOGY COMPARISON
Topology	Arrangement	Advantages	Disadvantages
Bus	All nodes share one common backbone cable with terminators at both ends.	Simple; least cabling; inexpensive.	One cable fault can stop the whole network; difficult to troubleshoot.
Star	Every node connects to a central hub/switch.	Easy to add/remove nodes; one node fault does not affect others.	Central device failure stops the network; more cable is needed.
Ring	Nodes form a closed loop; data passes node to node.	Orderly, predictable data flow; no collisions with token passing.	A single break can halt the ring; adding nodes disrupts it.
Mesh	Every node links directly to every other node.	Highly reliable; multiple paths; fault tolerant.	Very expensive; large amount of cabling.
Tree	Groups of star networks joined to a bus backbone.	Scalable; suitable for large structured networks.	Backbone failure affects large sections; complex.
Hybrid	Combination of two or more topologies.	Flexible; can be tailored to needs.	Design and management are complex and costly.




PROCEDURE
1.Place the LAN Trainer Kit on the bench, connect its power supply and switch it on. Confirm that the indicator lamps/LEDs are functioning.
2.Identify the individual node modules, central hub/switch module, backbone connector and supplied patch cords.
3.Bus topology: connect all node modules along the common backbone line and fit terminators at both ends.
4.Send data from one node and observe that the signal travels along the shared backbone and is seen by all nodes; only the addressed node accepts it.
5.Disconnect the backbone at a midpoint and note that communication beyond the break stops.
6.Star topology: connect each node module by a separate patch cord to a port on the central hub/switch module.
7.Transmit from one node and observe that data passes through the central module to reach the destination node.
8.Disconnect one node's cable and confirm that other nodes continue communicating. Then power off the central module and note that the whole network stops.
9.Ring topology: wire the node modules in a closed loop, connecting the output of each node to the input of the next until the last node connects back to the first.
10.Send data and observe it circulating from node to node until it reaches the intended destination.
11.Open the loop at one link and observe that the ring's data flow is interrupted.
12.Mesh topology: using the available patch cords, connect each node module directly to every other node module.
13.Transmit between two nodes and observe that multiple direct paths allow communication to continue even if one link is removed.
14.For each topology, record the number of cables used, the path taken by the data, and the effect of a single link or node failure.

OBSERVATION TABLE
The table below gives the general cable count and behaviour for the topologies described in the manual.
